---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../data/site';

const entry = page('/docs/privacy/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="When something is wrong">
  <p>
    This is the technical version: what leaves your device, when, where it goes, and what is stored
    on the way. The{' '}
    <a href="/privacy/">site privacy page</a> covers this website; this page covers the app.
  </p>

  <h2>The one sentence that matters</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Nothing leaves your iPhone until you add a destination and confirm it.</strong> There is
    no default destination, no hosted relay, and no server belonging to the maintainer. A default
    would be a place someone else chose, so there isn't one.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaves, and when</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Only to destinations you configured.</strong> Each destination can be limited to
      specific Health types, so a destination receives what you scoped it to and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only when its cadence says so</strong>, or when you tap Sync now, or when a Shortcut
      runs. A destination set to manual sends nothing on its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A connection test carries no Health data.</strong> It is a small probe in the
      destination's own format, and for line protocol it is a single point tagged{' '}
      <code>type=hozz_connection_test</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing else makes a network request.</strong> There is no analytics endpoint, no
      crash reporter, no update check, and no feature flag service — so there is no request to
      inspect and find innocuous.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is stored on the phone</h2>

  <p>
    Cursors, coverage state, destination configuration, delivery receipts, and bounded spool
    artifacts. <strong>Hozz does not keep a permanent local mirror of your Health history by default</strong>
    — it reads from Health, stages what it is about to send, and lets that go.
  </p>

  <p>
    Health-derived files are protected with complete-unless-open file protection and{' '}
    <strong>excluded from device backups</strong>, including SQLite side files and spool files. An
    export you deliberately saved is yours and lives wherever you saved it.
  </p>

  <h2>Credentials</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Destination credentials live in the device Keychain</strong> with{' '}
      <code>ThisDeviceOnly</code> accessibility, and are <strong>not synchronised</strong>. They are
      scoped to one destination and are never handed to a redirect that leaves the host you set up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Mac receiver token is the deliberate exception.</strong> It is shared through your
      own iCloud Keychain access group, so a phone and Mac signed into the same Apple ID can find
      each other without you copying a token by hand. Two devices on one Apple account are already
      known to belong to the same person. If that path is unavailable, Hozz falls back to pairing
      over the local network.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What never reaches a log</h2>

  <p>
    Logs and diagnostics must not include Health sample values, credentials, or secret destination
    details. This is enforced by tests rather than by intent.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why response bodies are thrown away">
    <p>
      When an endpoint rejects a batch it frequently echoes the offending record back. So Hozz
      records the status code and a human-readable failure state and <strong>discards the body</strong>
      — which is mildly inconvenient when debugging your own server, and the correct trade when the
      body may contain a Health value.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What Hozz cannot promise</h2>

  <p>
    Once data reaches a destination, that copy is yours to look after, and Hozz has no further say.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A server you point it at holds your Health data.</strong> If it is on the public
      internet, it should be behind TLS and authentication, and its operator — probably you —
      decides what happens next.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A cloud-hosted assistant connected to <a href="/docs/mcp/">the MCP server</a> may
        upload whatever it reads.</strong> That is the assistant's behaviour, and no local
      read-only server can prevent it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A synced folder goes wherever that folder syncs.</strong> Choosing iCloud Drive or
      Dropbox means choosing their handling of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>InfluxDB deduplicates</strong>, which is a data-fidelity limitation rather than a
      privacy one, but it is the same category of thing: the destination's behaviour is the
      destination's.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Choosing destinations you actually control is the whole discipline this app is built around, and
    it is the one part it cannot do for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Hozz never writes back into Apple Health</h2>

  <p>
    Not as a missing feature — as a decision. Health permanently attributes every sample to the app
    that wrote it, so anything Hozz imported would be stamped as Hozz's forever, destroying the
    provenance an archive exists to preserve. And HealthKit has no upsert, so importing the same
    export twice silently doubles it.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/data-coverage/#what-hozz-will-never-do">The full reasoning is on Data coverage.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>How to check any of this</h2>

  <p>
    Everything above is a negative claim, and a negative claim cannot be demonstrated by using the
    app — only by reading it. Hozz is GPL-3.0 and developed in the open, and the behaviour described
    here is meant to be proven by tests rather than by this page.{' '}
    <a href={GITHUB_URL}>Read the source.</a>
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
